WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:04.040
Welcome to PyMOL 101 with Molecular Memory!

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:09.680
I’m KP and in this lesson, we’ll learn
to use color in PyMOL.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:17.000
By default, PyMOL applies a version of CPK
coloring, where oxygen is red, nitrogen is

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:23.490
blue, sulfur is yellow, and phosphorous is
orange.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:29.200
Sometimes it’s useful to override this,
especially to look at the overall structure

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.900
of a macromolecule.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:36.550
we’ll also explore transparency in this
lesson to make our colors shine through.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.790
Let’s do this.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:44.530
Phosphofructokinase—PFK for short—is a
protein that speeds up a chemical reaction,

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:46.770
so it’s an enzyme.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:54.290
It’s the third enzyme in glycolysis, a 10-step
pathway that breaks down the carbohydrate

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:55.590
glucose.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:01:02.850
PFK is cool because it can interconvert between
two shapes, or conformations, depending on

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:05.689
chemical signals in the cell.

00:01:05.689 --> 00:01:11.210
One conformation speeds up the reaction more
efficiently than the other, allowing this

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:18.420
enzyme to act as a main control valve determining
whether we break down carbohydrates for energy,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:23.030
otherwise we can store them or make bigger
molecules in biosynthesis.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:28.290
Load the biological assembly of 6pfk in PyMOL.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:35.560
File, get PDB, 6pfk, assembly 1.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.280
Display the sequence, and let’s scroll through
it.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:44.540
We see breaks in the amino acid sequences,
and this is because PFK has four chains of

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:45.710
amino acids.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:50.680
It’s a multichain protein with quaternary
structure.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:56.719
This particular structure is the less active
form of PFK, and each subunit is bound to

00:01:56.719 --> 00:01:57.920
PGA.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:06.590
We’re not focusing on water molecules in
this lesson, so let’s hide them: H, waters.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:09.890
Let’s color the chains to see them easily.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:13.980
In the names panel, click the C button for
color.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.330
Choose by chain, and then the top choice.

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:24.560
This star (*) elem C means we’re applying
CPK coloring to show the elements.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:30.569
Now, in the sequence, select a PGA and zoom
in on it using the A button.

00:02:30.569 --> 00:02:35.540
Notice the CPK coloring—this orange atom
here is phosphorous.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.920
Zoom back out using 6pfk to view the whole
protein.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.510
And I don’t want this selection to get in
the way of my next steps, so I’m gonna go

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:46.130
ahead and use the A button to delete it.

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:48.720
Let’s add a surface rendering.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:55.650
Names panel, S button, surface - to layer
a surface rendering on top of the default

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:56.650
cartoon.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:03.290
With CPK coloring applied, the surface is
busy, detracting from the chain coloring.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:07.720
Surface may be a rendering where CPK coloring
is less preferred.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:13.069
Click C, by chain, and select the last choice.

00:03:13.069 --> 00:03:18.459
This colors each chain a solid color, without
CPK coloring.

00:03:18.459 --> 00:03:21.690
Now we’re really highlighting the four chains
of PFK.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:27.440
Now, let’s color by ss – secondary structure.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:33.099
This highlights alpha helices, beta sheets,
and areas of the protein without a defined

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:36.380
secondary structure called loops.

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:41.870
But this rendering isn’t super useful with
the protein shown as surface, since the bumpy

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:46.000
surface obscures the secondary structure.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.000
Hide the surface to reveal the cartoon beneath.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.650
Now, that’s cool, and very useful.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:58.270
What if we want to show both the secondary
structure /and/ the protein’s surface?

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:02.670
We can make the surface semi-transparent to
achieve this!

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:06.250
Add the surface back, using the show button.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:15.079
Now, use the dropdown setting menu, find transparency,
surface, and let’s go with 60%.

00:04:15.079 --> 00:04:21.400
Now, let’s make the surface stand out a
little better by changing its color.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:30.479
C, by rep, surface, and let’s choose one
of these grays.

00:04:30.479 --> 00:04:36.009
The secondary structure of the multicolor
cartoon we see beneath is determined by hydrogen

00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:42.419
bonds between the amino acid backbone atoms,
which are called “main chain” in the PyMOL

00:04:42.419 --> 00:04:43.419
menus.

00:04:43.419 --> 00:04:50.009
But, we can also see the bumpiness of the
surface—that where amino acid side chains

00:04:50.009 --> 00:04:51.580
are taking up space.

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:53.759
Let’s add these side chains.

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:56.949
Show, side chains, sticks.

00:04:56.949 --> 00:05:01.230
And we can now see how these fit into the
surface.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:06.020
The sticks are kind of thick and the rendering
is looking much too busy, so let’s hide

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:07.020
the sticks.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:13.590
Now, let’s show lines instead and color
them with CPK coloring.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:16.570
Now choose C, by element.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:23.280
Each of these choices, and there are several
sets of them, colors by a modified CPK coloring,

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.480
where we choose the carbon color.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:31.540
Carbons don’t have to be gray or black,
as they are in traditional CPK coloring.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:35.850
Choose either cyan or white carbons from this
first set.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:40.819
Cyan is a light blue green color that’s
relatively colorblind friendly.

00:05:40.819 --> 00:05:46.460
When choosing the carbon color, it’s important
to think about the atoms in your structure.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:52.740
When modeling proteins, we want to avoid colors
that are similar to the blue, red, and yellow

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:59.580
of CPK nitrogen, oxygen, and sulfur, because
we find these in amino acids.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.990
Next we’ll play with the rendering of PGA.

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:04.860
But before we do that: a quick terminology
break.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:11.789
We mentioned this is the less active conformation,
and PGA helps force the enzyme into this shape

00:06:11.789 --> 00:06:14.430
because it’s an inhibitor.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:19.289
Inhibitors slow or stop an enzyme from performing
a chemical reaction.

00:06:19.289 --> 00:06:25.169
And anything that binds a protein can be generally
called a ligand, or lie-gand.

00:06:25.169 --> 00:06:28.509
So, the inhibitor is a ligand.

00:06:28.509 --> 00:06:33.990
Let’s try a new representation, to gain
a sense of where the ligands bind to the protein.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:38.400
Using the sequence, select all four PGAs.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:43.180
You can click on each individually in the
sequence, or click and drag over all four

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:44.360
at once.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:51.920
The letters Sele come up in the names panel,
and next to that, let’s click, A, rename,

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:54.400
and we’ll call these ligands.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.360
Now, show these as spheres.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:01.810
Remember, “show as” changes the rendering
completely.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:08.400
By contrast, plain old “show” lets you
layer representations on top of each other,

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.849
like we have with the stick and surface views
of the protein.

00:07:11.849 --> 00:07:15.560
Cool, the location of the ligands is easy
to see now.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:21.729
Let’s make their carbon atoms contrast the
protein a bit more, but still color by element.

00:07:21.729 --> 00:07:27.000
C, by element, and choose one with green carbon
atoms.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:30.260
The cartoon is another rendering that can
have transparency.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:36.250
Let’s hide the lines and surface, go back
to cartoon rendering, and apply transparency.

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:40.360
This is a good time to use “show as”.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:44.240
6pfk, show as, cartoon.

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:48.789
Notice this command cleared both the lines
and the surface.

00:07:48.789 --> 00:07:51.940
But it also cleared the sphere rendering of
the ligand.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:58.240
No problem, that’s why we made a selection
for the object we’re focusing on.

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:02.910
Next to ligands, S, spheres to get the ligand
back.

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:09.729
Now, Setting dropdown, Transparency, Cartoon,
50%.

00:08:09.729 --> 00:08:14.319
And we can now see both secondary structure
and the location of the ligand.

00:08:14.319 --> 00:08:19.370
The type of rendering you choose for a model
really depends on what you want to show the

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:20.370
viewer.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:24.840
It’s a good idea to think about this before
starting the model, but don’t be afraid

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:29.139
to play around with the renderings to find
the best display possible.

00:08:29.139 --> 00:08:32.780
Thanks for watching this episode of PyMOL
101.

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:38.700
Next time, we’ll refresh and build on techniques
from Lesson 2 to show and label a binding

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:41.099
site on PFK.

00:08:41.099 --> 00:08:46.180
If you’re enjoying this series, like, subscribe,
and if there’s anything specific you’d

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:48.339
like to learn, let me know in the comments!

